    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "AAAAI3//+Q0\u003d",
        "filename": "keystone/middleware/auth_token.py",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 401,
      "author": {
        "id": 2218
      },
      "writtenOn": "2012-06-22T19:18:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We had a few discussions.  The thing is,  the service can happily go on using the signed tokens the same as the existing tokens, but the reverse is not true.  So if we start using signed tokens,  we need to ensure that every token is a signed token.\n\nThe change made this way will update the remote systems once they import the new auth_token.py file,  preventing it to require human intervention in changing the paste config files by hand.  I think this is the right approach.",
      "parentUuid": "AAAAI3///Kk\u003d",
      "revId": "a525587251d0904964afe6ab831a1ce881160293",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "AAAAI3//59g\u003d",
        "filename": "keystone/middleware/auth_token.py",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 401,
      "author": {
        "id": 154
      },
      "writtenOn": "2012-06-28T18:30:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "One way to abstract the fact that a token is signed or not is to leave the auth_token filter as was before, and create another PKI filter that sits in front of the auth_token filter.  The PKI filter will inspect and see if the token is indeed signed.  If it is, then it will go on and verify it and short-circuit the auth_token filter.  If it is not, then it will just pass the token along to the auth_token filter to verify it the old-fashioned way.",
      "parentUuid": "AAAAI3//+Q0\u003d",
      "revId": "a525587251d0904964afe6ab831a1ce881160293",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
